s-Menten hypothesis?"}, {"back": "Serum glutamate-pyruvate transaminase (SGPT).", "front": "What was the former name of ALT?"}, {"back": "Carbonic acid.", "front": "Which substrate is acted upon by carbonic anhydrase?"}, {"back": "Malonate is a structural analog of succinate.", "front": "What is the structural relationship between malonate and succinate?"}, {"back": "By taking the reciprocal of the Michaelis-Menten equation.", "front": "How is the Lineweaver-Burk plot derived?"}, {"back": "GTP or ATP.", "front": "Which nucleoside triphosphates can drive succinyl-CoA synthesis?"}, {"back": "It induces a structural change that fits the substrate and excludes water.", "front": "What happens when D-glucose binds to hexokinase?"}, {"back": "Transaminases.", "front": "What is another name for aminotransferases?"}, {"back": "Oxidation of lactate to pyruvate with reduction of NAD+ to NADH + H+.", "front": "What reaction is catalyzed by lactate dehydrogenase?"}, {"back": "Alpha-amylase.", "front": "Which enzyme hydrolyzes alpha-1,4 glycosidic bonds?"}, {"back": "Removal of hydrogen atoms or hydride ions.", "front": "What is the function of dehydrogenases?"}, {"back": "The region of an enzyme where substrates bind and catalysis occurs.", "front": "What is the active site?"}, {"back": "Glucokinase, hexokinase, glucosidase, and urease.", "front": "Give examples of enzymes named according to their substrates."}, {"back": "Taq polymerase.", "front": "Which enzyme is used in PCR because of its high-temperature activity?"}, {"back": "Specificity of an enzyme for its required cofactor in addition to its substrate.", "front": "What is cofactor specificity?"}, {"back": "Reduced lipoyl groups transfer hydrogen to FAD, forming FADH2.", "front": "What happens during lipoic acid regeneration in PDHc?"}, {"back": "They are used as diagnostic tools for many diseases.", "front": "What is a clinical use of enzymes?"}, {"back": "26,000 calories/mol.", "front": "What activation energy is required for uncatalyzed sucrose hydrolysis?"}, {"back": "Parallel lines.", "front": "What is the Lineweaver-Burk pattern of uncompetitive inhibition?"}, {"back": "Conversion of substrates into products through binding at the enzyme's active site.", "front": "What is enzyme catalysis?"}, {"back": "Interconversion of aldoses and ketoses.", "front": "What do aldose-ketose isomerases catalyze?"}, {"back": "Flexibility of the active-site region.", "front": "What is the main feature of the induced-fit model?"}, {"back": "Flavin adenine dinucleotide.", "front": "What is FAD?"}, {"back": "Amino, carboxyl, and thiol groups.", "front": "Which functional groups can participate in substrate binding at the active site?"}, {"back": "v = Vmax[S] / Km.", "front": "What is the rate equation when [S] is much lower than Km?"}, {"back": "Active site, apoenzyme, cofactor, coenzyme, and substrate.", "front": "What are the main components of an enzyme?"}, {"back": "Pepsin and trypsin.", "front": "Give examples of simple enzymes."}, {"back": "Five, LDH1 to LDH5.", "front": "How many LDH isozymes are present?"}, {"back": "Hydrolysis of ester bonds.", "front": "What do lipases and esterases catalyze?"}, {"back": "EC 1.1.1.27.", "front": "What is the EC number of LDH?"}, {"back": "Enzymes can be proteins.", "front": "What did Sumner demonstrate by crystallizing urease?"}, {"back": "It increases apparent Km.", "front": "What is the effect of competitive inhibition on apparent Km?"}, {"back": "Permanent enzyme inactivation without inhibitor release.", "front": "What characterizes irreversible enzyme inhibition?"}, {"back": "Formation of L-malate with 100% stereospecificity.", "front": "What is the stereochemical outcome of fumarase-catalyzed hydration?"}, {"back": "The amount of functional enzyme decreases.", "front": "Why does Vmax decrease during irreversible inhibition?"}, {"back": "L-amino acids.", "front": "Which amino acids are acted upon by L-amino acid oxidase?"}, {"back": "Two to four.", "front": "How many Ni2+ ions are present per urease active site according to the lecture?"}, {"back": "Aspartate aminotransferase.", "front": "What does AST stand for?"}, {"back": "The chemical reaction catalyzed by an enzyme.", "front": "What does an enzyme commission (EC) number describe?"}, {"back": "By increasing substrate concentration.", "front": "How can competitive inhibition be overcome?"}, {"back": "EC 1.2.3.4.", "front": "What is the EC number of oxalate oxidase?"}, {"back": "No.", "front": "Can an uncompetitive inhibitor bind to free enzyme?"}, {"back": "Inversion of configuration at one specific asymmetric carbon in molecules with multiple chiral centers.", "front": "What do epimerases catalyze?"}, {"back": "The active site of the free enzyme.", "front": "Where does a competitive inhibitor bind?"}, {"back": "Reactants must overcome an energy barrier before forming products.", "front": "Why do chemical reactions require activation energy?"}, {"back": "Formation of carbon–nitrogen bonds.", "front": "What do EC 6.3 ligases catalyze?"}, {"back": "The enzyme's tertiary structure.", "front": "What determines the three-dimensional shape of the active site?"}, {"back": "Formation of a transient covalent enzyme-substrate intermediate.", "front": "What is covalent catalysis?"}, {"back": "Stabilization of charged transition states through electrostatic interactions at the active site.", "front": "What is electrostatic catalysis?"}, {"back": "EC 5.1.", "front": "What is the EC subclass of racemases and epimerases?"}, {"back": "It dissociates from the enzyme in a changed form.", "front": "What happens to a cosubstrate after catalysis?"}, {"back": "Absolute specificity.", "front": "What is another name for substrate specificity?"}, {"back": "They increase reaction rates by lowering activation energy.", "front": "What is the main effect of catalytic mechanisms?"}, {"back": "It affects molecular kinetic energy and the stability of the enzyme-substrate complex.", "front": "How does temperature affect Km?"}, {"back": "Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP).", "front": "Which coenzyme is required by aminotransferases?"}, {"back": "Intracellular hydrolytic destruction of materials not needed by the cell.", "front": "What is the function of lysosomal enzymes?"}, {"back": "Catalysis involving proton donors or acceptors provided by active-site groups.", "front": "What is general acid-base catalysis?"}, {"back": "An inhibitory peptide segment blocks the active site or prevents proper folding.", "front": "How does a zymogen remain inactive?"}, {"back": "Cleavage of phosphodiester bonds.", "front": "What do phosphodiesterases catalyze?"}, {"back": "No, they remain unchanged.", "front": "Are enzymes consumed during reactions?"}, {"back": "It abstracts a proton and activates water.", "front": "What is the role of histidine in carbonic anhydrase?"}, {"back": "C–N peptide bonds.", "front": "What do peptidases and proteases hydrolyze?"}, {"back": "Daniel Koshland.", "front": "Who proposed the induced-fit model?"}, {"back": "From the reaction, substrate, source, regulation, or trivial naming.", "front": "How can recommended enzyme names be derived?"}, {"back": "Succinate + CoA-SH + GTP → Succinyl-CoA + GDP + Pi.", "front": "What is the synthetic reaction of succinyl-CoA synthetase?"}, {"back": "L-glutamine.", "front": "What is the product of glutamine synthetase?"}, {"back": "Vitamin B5.", "front": "Which vitamin is the precursor of CoA?"}, {"back": "A region that stabilizes the negative charge on the substrate's carbonyl oxygen during catalysis.", "front": "What is the oxyanion hole?"}, {"back": "The genetic code in DNA.", "front": "What determines the amino acid sequence of an enzyme?"}, {"back": "Fisher model or rigid model.", "front": "What is another name for the lock-and-key model?"}, {"back": "DNA.", "front": "What carries the information required for enzyme synthesis?"}, {"back": "The selective ability of an enzyme to catalyze a particular reaction involving specific substrates.", "front": "What is enzyme specificity?"}, {"back": "Half Vmax.", "front": "What is the reaction velocity when [S] = Km?"}, {"back": "Noncompetitive allosteric inhibition.", "front": "What type of inhibition does isoleucine exert on threonine deaminase?"}, {"back": "Yes.", "front": "Can an allosteric enzyme have multiple allosteric sites?"}, {"back": "It typically inhibits that step.", "front": "What is the effect of excess end product on the first irreversible step of a pathway?"}, {"back": "Noncovalent interactions.", "front": "What type of bonds mediate allosteric effector binding?"}, {"back": "Acetylcholinesterase (AChE).", "front": "Which enzyme is irreversibly inhibited by organophosphates?"}, {"back": "They covalently bind to a reactive serine hydroxyl group in the active site.", "front": "How do organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase?"}, {"back": "Threonine.", "front": "Which amino acid is converted into isoleucine through a short enzymatic pathway?"}, {"back": "Regulation in which the end product of a metabolic pathway controls its own synthesis.", "front": "What is feedback regulation?"}, {"back": "A molecule that binds an allosteric site and alters the shape of the active site.", "front": "What is a modulator?"}, {"back": "A regulatory allosteric site separate from the catalytic site.", "front": "Where does the end product bind during feedback inhibition?"}, {"back": "It allows rapid regulation according to cellular isoleucine levels.", "front": "What is the benefit of reversible feedback inhibition in isoleucine synthesis?"}, {"back": "Threonine dehydratase.", "front": "Which enzyme catalyzes the first step of isoleucine biosynthesis?"}, {"back": "A molecule that binds the allosteric site and changes the active-site shape to facilitate substrate binding.", "front": "What is a positive allosteric modulator?"}, {"back": "Activators and inhibitors.", "front": "What are the two types of allosteric effectors?"}, {"back": "It inhibits enzyme activity.", "front": "What is the effect of a negative modulator?"}, {"back": "A molecule that binds the allosteric site and changes the active-site shape to prevent substrate binding.", "front": "What is a negative allosteric modulator?"}, {"back": "As a negative allosteric effector.", "front": "How does an end product act in feedback inhibition?"}, {"back": "It induces conformational changes that increase or decrease activity.", "front": "How does allosteric binding affect enzyme activity?"}, {"back": "Regulation of enzyme activity through binding of molecules to a site separate from the active site.", "front": "What is allosteric regulation?"}, {"back": "A regulatory allosteric site distinct from the catalytic site.", "front": "Where does isoleucine bind to threonine deaminase?"}, {"back": "It adjusts metabolic activity according to cellular needs and maintains homeostasis.", "front": "What is the physiological importance of allosteric regulation?"}, {"back": "It activates the enzyme.", "front": "What is the effect of a positive modulator?"}, {"back": "Threonine deaminase.", "front": "What is another name for threonine dehydratase?"}, {"back": "Cyclooxygenase (COX).", "front": "Which enzyme is irreversibly inhibited by aspirin?"}, {"back": "Noncompetitive inhibitors.", "front": "How are negative modulators classified in the lecture?"}, {"back": "It covalently acetylates a key serine residue.", "front": "How does aspirin inhibit COX?"}, {"back": "Pyruvate dehydrogenase.", "front": "Which enzyme is inhibited by ATP, acetyl-CoA, NADH, and fatty acids according to the lecture?"}, {"back": "Isoleucine.", "front": "Which molecule inhibits threonine deaminase?"}, {"back": "Prostaglandin and thromboxane synthesis.", "front": "What synthesis pathways are blocked by aspirin's COX inhibition?"}, {"back": "Through layered partial or cooperative feedback inhibition.", "front": "How are feedback loops regulated in complex or branched metabolic pathways?"}, {"back": "A conformational change reduces or stops enzyme activity.", "front": "What happens after end-product binding during feedback inhibition?"}, {"back": "Yes.", "front": "Are allosteric interactions reversible?"}, {"back": "Excess of one end product inhibits its own branch without shutting down parallel branches.", "front": "How does feedback inhibition work in a branched metabolic pathway?"}, {"back": "Yes.", "front": "Is feedback inhibition by isoleucine reversible?"}, {"back": "The remaining uninhibited enzymes retain their original substrate affinity.", "front": "Why does Km remain unchanged during irreversible inhibition?"}, {"back": "To ensure end products are produced only when needed.", "front": "What is the purpose of feedback regulation?"}, {"back": "Sarin gas and DIPF.", "front": "Give examples of organophosphate inhibitors."}, {"back": "At least two: an active site and an allosteric site.", "front": "How many types of binding sites do allosteric enzymes have?"}, {"back": "It regulates substrate flow according to product requirements.", "front": "How does feedback inhibition help maintain metabolic balance?"}, {"back": "A regulatory site distinct from the catalytic active site.", "front": "What is an allosteric site?"}, {"back": "The first committed or irreversible step unique to the pathway.", "front": "Which enzyme step is typically targeted by feedback inhibition?"}]	2026-11-07 15:46:29.341106+00	deck	f9f3f8e0-61e6-410e-a4d1-ab49c3b03b5a
